## Handling User Interactions

Charts can be made interactive by enabling [tooltips](./tooltips/), [highlighting](./highlighting/) series, and responding to [events](./events/).

### Customizing Tooltips

Tooltips are enabled by default. Their content is based on the data values and keys of the series.

Use the `tooltip.renderer` callback function to customize the content of the tooltip.

```js
tooltip: {
    renderer: function ({ datum, xKey, yKey }) {
        return {
            heading: 'Clothing Production',
            title: yName.toUpperCase(),
            data: [
                {
                    label: datum[xKey],
                    value: datum[yKey].toFixed(1),
                },
            ],
        };
    },
},
```

Use the following [CSS Classes](./tooltips/#using-css-styles) to customize the appearance of the tooltip:

```css
.ag-charts-tooltip,
.ag-charts-tooltip-heading,
.ag-charts-tooltip-title,
.ag-charts-tooltip-label ,
.ag-charts-tooltip-value {
    ...
}
```

You can also return HTML to create a [Custom Tooltip](./tooltips/#using-custom-tooltips):

```js
{
    renderer: function (params) {
        return (
            '<div class="custom-tooltip">' +
                params.datum[params.xKey]
            '</div>'
        );
    },
},
```

### Highlighting Hovered Data

Items and Series can be [highlighted](./series-highlighting/) when hovered over. Item highlighting (e.g. markers) is enabled by default.

Item and Series highlighting styles can be [customized](./series-highlighting/#customisation) with the `highlightStyles` property:

```js
theme: {
    overrides: {
        bar: {
            series: {
                highlightStyle: {
                    // Attributes that apply to individual items within a single series.
                    item: {
                    fill: 'red',
                    stroke: 'maroon',
                    strokeWidth: 4,
                    },
                    // Attributes that apply to the entire series containing the highlighted item.
                    series: {
                        dimOpacity: 0.2,
                        strokeWidth: 2
                    }
                }
            },
        },
    },
},
```

### Handling Events

Callbacks are provided for various [Series](./events/#reference-AgSeriesListeners), [Legend](./events/#reference-AgChartLegendListeners) and [Chart](./events/#reference-AgBaseChartListeners) events.

Listen for [Events](./react/events/) with the `listeners` property:

```js
{
    // Charts Events
    listeners: {
        click: (_event) => {
            console.log('click');
        },
        doubleClick: (_event) => {
            console.log('double click');
        },
        seriesNodeClick: ({ datum, xKey, yKey, seriesId }) => {
            console.log('node clicked')
        },
        seriesVisibilityChange: ({ seriesId, visible }: AgSeriesVisibilityChange) => {
            console.log('series visibility changed')
        },
        zoom: (event) => {
            console.log('zoom level changed')
        },
    },
    // Series Events
    series: [
        {
            listeners: {
                nodeClick: (event) => {
                    console.log('node clicked')
                },
            },
        },
    ],
    // Legend Events
    legend: {
        listeners: {
            legendItemClick: ({ seriesId, itemId, enabled }: AgChartLegendClickEvent) => {
                console.log('item clicked')
            },
        },
    }
}
```

### Saving and Restoring State

The [State](./state/) of the chart can be saved and restored using the `getState()` and `saveState()` APIs:

```js
function saveState() {
    const newState = chart.getState();
    // save to database...
}

function restoreState() {
    // retrieve state from database...
    chart.setState(state);
}
```

To set the [initial state](./api-state/#initial-state) of the chart, use the `initialState` property:

```js
initialState: {
    zoom: {
        rangeX: {
            start: {
                __type: 'date',
                value: new Date('2021-01-01').getTime(),
            },
        },
    },
    ...
}
```

